          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining multiple search methods to maximize relevance \\u2014 lexical and semantic retrieval merged via ranking fusion.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single detailed source; hybrid retrieval is production-standard in RAG systems, but additional corroboration would strengthen confidence.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines **sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) with **dense vector search**, then merges the two ranked result sets\\u2014often via **reciprocal rank fusion**\\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Performance Profile\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries mixing:\\n- **Rare identifiers** (error codes, product SKUs) \\u2014 where lexical search excels\\n- **Natural language intent** \\u2014 where semantic search excels\\n\\nThis balanced approach has become the **default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems**.\\n\\n### Merging Strategy\\n\\n**Reciprocal rank fusion** is the standard technique for combining the two ranked sets, because it avoids score normalization issues across different rankers (BM25 scores and vector similarity scores use incompatible scales).\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval synthesis, eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-08\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
